cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "two-clocks: a bitemporal retrieval benchmark"
message: "If you use this benchmark or its closed-form results, please cite it."
type: dataset
authors:
  - given-names: Yuncong
    family-names: Liu
    email: yuncongliu0703@gmail.com
date-released: "2026-08-10"
url: "https://github.com/ycsama0703/two-clocks"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - bitemporal
  - as-of retrieval
  - look-ahead bias
  - temporal identifiability
  - long-context retrieval
  - relative position encoding
abstract: >-
  A financial fact carries two clocks: the period it is about (event time) and
  the moment it first became knowable (availability time). Existing long-context
  and temporal retrieval benchmarks are uni-temporal, assuming text order carries
  the complete temporal signal - an assumption that holds only because synthetic
  corpora are generated in temporal order. This benchmark supplies 21,268 real
  collision pairs from SEC EDGAR, where two versions of the same figure share an
  event time but differ in availability, and shows that for any single linear
  order the two-dimensional state is many-to-one, so as-of eligibility is not
  recoverable from a relative position representation. Degradation is given in
  closed form and verified bit-exact against a deployed RoPE implementation.
